<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our investigation of Pipeline-Parallel Self-Speculative Decoding (PPSD) demonstrates that fully pipelining draft and verification stages can substantially mitigate the cost of invalid speculative tokens, yielding consistent <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.01\times\sim 3.81\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.01</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">×</mo><mo lspace="0em">∼</mo><mn>3.81</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.01\times\sim 3.81\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedups across diverse LLMs and tasks.
However, several practical considerations and limitations warrant further discussion.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Limitations</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Infrastructure Requirements.</span>
PPSD relies on a fine-grained pipeline-parallel deployment across multiple GPUs (or pipeline workers), which may not be available in all inference-serving environments, e.g. resource limited scenarios.
Small-scale or single-GPU deployments cannot fully exploit the parallelism advantages and may see only marginal gains.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pipeline Granularity Trade-offs.</span>
Determining the optimal number of layers per pipeline stage (i.e., the exit depth <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and the number of workers) requires careful tuning: too coarse-grained a pipeline limits overlap opportunities, while too fine-grained a pipeline can introduce communication overhead and imbalance.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bounded by Acceptance Rate.</span>
Although PPSD decouples draft length <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> from throughput degradation, poor alignment (low <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>) still reduces effective speedup (see in <a href="#S4.Ex2" title="In 4.2 Verify-While-Draft Decoding ‣ 4 Methods ‣ PPSD: Pipeline Parallelism is All You Need for Optimized Early-Exit Based Self-Speculative Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), since verification must proceed for nearly every draft token.
Improvements in early-exit head accuracy (e.g., via stronger distillation in <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Design of Self-distillation Loss Function ‣ Appendix C Training Details ‣ PPSD: Pipeline Parallelism is All You Need for Optimized Early-Exit Based Self-Speculative Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>) remain critical.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Architecture Constraints.</span>
Our design targets decoder<span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>only transformer architectures with homogeneous layer structures.
Extending PPSD to encoder–decoder models or heterogeneous architectures may require nontrivial modifications to the pipeline schedule and exit-head design.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Applications</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">PPSD is particularly well-suited for high<span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>throughput LLM services —such as real<span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>time chatbots, summarization APIs, and code<span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>generation endpoints—where latency and cost per token are paramount.
By seamlessly integrating into existing decoding loops without altering the backbone weights or requiring separate draft models, PPSD can be adopted in production clusters to reduce GPU-hours and energy consumption.
Moreover, PPSD’s verify<span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>while<span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text">-</span>draft paradigm could be combined with complementary techniques—such as quantization, multiple token prediction, or dynamic token<span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text">-</span>level branching—to further compress inference time.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Future Direction.</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Looking ahead, we plan to explore adaptive pipeline reconfiguration: dynamically altering <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and the number of pipeline stages based on runtime acceptance statistics, input complexity, or hardware availability.
Another promising avenue is multi-exit speculative decoding, where multiple early-exit heads at different depths collaborate to adaptively adjust the draft length on a per-token basis. When integrated with PPSD, this approach can further reduce the number of discarded draft tokens by dynamically selecting the early-exit head with the highest expected acceptance rate at each step.
Finally, integrating soft<span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>verification mechanisms—where partial token probabilities guide speculations—may further improve the acceptance rate <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and unlock additional speedups without sacrificing output quality.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Execution of PPSD</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pipeline Based Self-Verification Mechanism</span> in <a href="#alg1" title="In Appendix B Execution of PPSD ‣ PPSD: Pipeline Parallelism is All You Need for Optimized Early-Exit Based Self-Speculative Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> implements our distributed inference framework using a multi-stage pipeline architecture for token generation and verification.
Beginning with input sequence <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, the algorithm performs iterative token generation through coordinated computation across <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> GPU devices. The core workflow comprises three phases:</p>
<ol id="A2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Master control initialization</span>: GPU<sub id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sub">0</sub> initiates generation and performs first-stage inference, establishing the pipeline via cross-device activation passing.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intermediate processing</span>: Nodes GPU<sub id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sub">1</sub> to GPU<sub id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">N-1</span></sub> progressively process features, generating verification tokens <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i+1}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i+1}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> upon detecting early-exit conditions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Final verification</span>: GPU<sub id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">N-1</span></sub> serves as the terminal verification layer, transmitting either:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{TokenType}=\begin{cases}\text{standard tokens}&amp;\text{if }\text{{not\quad has\_early\_exited}}\\
\text{check tokens}&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>TokenType</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>standard tokens</mtext></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">not has_early_exited</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>check tokens</mtext></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{TokenType}=\begin{cases}\text{standard tokens}&amp;\text{if }\text{{not\quad has\_early\_exited}}\\
\text{check tokens}&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Pipeline Based Self-Verification Mechanism</figcaption>
<p id="alg1.4" class="ltx_p ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom"><span id="alg1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> sequence <math id="alg1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{T}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<div id="alg1.5" class="ltx_listing ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
            
          <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}\leftarrow\{x_{T}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}\leftarrow\{x_{T}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
